{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And HaShem smelled, etc.</b> The intent of the statement is to say that even though the offering that Noah brought was from his own property — which is the property of the heavens that HaShem created in His world — nevertheless He was placated by them, and through the medium of their being offered He volunteered to do good and swore that He would never again destroy all flesh, without asking anything of him; rather, as it were, of His own accord He was placated, as though what He received belonged to the offerer and not to Himself. Along these lines my master and teacher, my lord my elder, of blessed memory, used to explain a certain statement (Eiruvin 65b), and this is their language: 'Whoever allows himself to be persuaded by his wine has within him something of the knowledge of his Maker, as it is said: וַיָּרַח ה׳ אֶת רֵיחַ הַנִּיחֹחַ' — end of quotation. He of blessed memory explained: whoever allows himself to be persuaded by his own wine, when a guest brings him a cup of wine from the host's own wine, and through it he grants him what he seeks from him of his own accord — he has within him something of the knowledge of his Maker, for thus did HaShem do, as I have explained just above. And he was precise in saying 'מִתְפַּתֶּה' (allows himself to be persuaded) to say that He was persuaded through the medium of what was presented to Him without needing anything at all. Accordingly, the verse's statement of this matter comes to instruct one who wishes to walk in the ways of his Maker, and also for our recognition that he has within him something of the knowledge of his Maker. And although we find in several places that the verse says 'רֵיחַ נִיחוֹחַ,' we do not find that He volunteered to do good of His own accord like this, but rather that He would be placated by it or forgive his sin." "<b>And HaShem said, etc., I will not again add, etc.</b> One must know why He repeated, saying <b>I will not add and I will not add.</b> And Rashi, of blessed memory, explained that He repeated the matter as an oath, etc., and so they expounded in tractate Shevuot (36b) — end. But it is difficult, for the words of our Sages of blessed memory are not so, and this is their language: Rabbi Elazar said, 'לָאו לָאו' (no, no) constitutes an oath, etc. Rava said: and that is when he says 'לָאו לָאו' two times, as it is written ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת עוֹד כָּל בָּשָׂר עוֹד מִמֵּי הַמַּבּוּל וְלֹא יִהְיֶה״ etc., and it is written (Genesis 9:15) ״וְלֹא יִהְיֶה עוֹד הַמַּיִם לְמַבּוּל״ — end. And these are two verses that appear in HaShem's words to Noah just below, and he did not prove it from our verse as per Rashi's words." "And the reason the Talmud did not prove the oath from the doubling of 'לֹא אוֹסִיף' and 'לֹא אוֹסִיף' stated in the first verse that HaShem spoke to His heart — there is a sound reason for this, for the two were not said regarding one specific matter such that they would count as a doubling. For the first 'לֹא אוֹסִיף' concerned the details of the earth, for it had been stricken two or three times because of the sons of man: man sinned — the earth was stricken; Cain sinned — the earth was stricken by not giving its yield; the generation of the Flood sinned — the earth was stricken. Therefore, at the beginning of His will, may He be blessed, He said 'I will not add to curse again the earth for the sake of man' — not even one part of the earth, nor any detail of its details. And further, HaShem desired, with the contentment of spirit that was then before Him, and said 'and I will not add again to strike every living thing,' as He had done, even without any destruction of the earth at all. If so, where is the place of the doubling to say that it constitutes an oath? Therefore Rava expounded from two verses, namely 'no longer shall all flesh be cut off... and there shall no longer be a flood' and 'the waters shall no longer be a flood' (Genesis 9:11). And examine the intent of the verse and the exposition of our Sages of blessed memory in their place, with the help of the Name. And now Rashi's words stand in need of examination. Perhaps he had a different version of the Talmud text, as the Rosh wrote there in Shevuot. And see what I explained on the verse ״וְלֹא יִכָּרֵת״ etc." "And it remains for us to give a reason why the verse varied in its expressions: once it put 'to curse' before the word 'עוֹד,' and once it put the word 'עוֹד' before 'to curse,' and did not make the wording uniform. The reason is that the curses with which the earth was cursed exist both for the past and for the future — they have not ceased from it. But as for the curse upon all living things — the dead have already died and are as though they never were, and the living bear no trace of what passed. Therefore HaShem was precise in His words and said <b>I will not add to curse again the earth</b> — [meaning] more curse upon the curse that is already upon it. And He placed the word 'עוֹד' after saying 'to curse,' to say: I will not add curse beyond what it already is. And this indicates that it still remains under the first curse. But regarding the striking of all flesh, He placed 'עוֹד' before the striking, for in the addition of this striking there is no portion of the first one. And examine this well." "<b>For the inclination of the heart, etc.</b> This is to be explained along the lines of what they said of blessed memory in tractate Bava Kamma (39a): an ox of the stadiums that gored is exempt, as it is written (Exodus 21:28) ״כִּי יִגַּח״ — and not that they make it gore — end of quotation. And so it is with man: from his youth, before he is discerning enough to reject evil and choose good, the evil inclination precedes him from the time he is young in his mother's womb, and the evil guides him in his discernment. And when he grows up, the evil has already imprinted itself in him, just as they imprint goring in an ox of the stadiums. And this reasoning serves to avoid judging him with utter extermination, but in any case he is still punished for not heeding the voice of Elohim, and he differs from a trained ox, for man's advantage over the beast is to reject evil upon recognizing it and to choose good. And the claim of 'evil from his youth' helped so that HaShem would not be as wrathful with him, for it appears that he did wonders [in sinning], since he has a reason to act wickedly on account of his being trained from his youth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {2 "The abbreviation השם is rendered HaShem per voice rules; where it appears in the phrase עֶזְרַת הַשֵּׁם ('with the help of the Name') it is kept as a reverent reference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:40:39.342461126Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}